Continue after monitor stubs when a frame is deoptimized.

Compiled code runs monitorenter and monitorexit in a shared stub after
taking the object off the expression stack. A thread can wait in that stub
for a contended monitor while another thread loads a class that overrides
a method the compiled code calls directly; the waiting frame is then
deoptimized. The stub still finishes the bytecode and returns to the
deoptimization entry, which ran the same bytecode again: monitorenter
popped an operand that was no longer there and moved the expression stack
past its bottom, and monitorexit would fail on the monitor it had just
released.

Continue at the next bytecode for a topmost frame stopped at monitorenter
or monitorexit.

--- a/cldc/src/vm/share/runtime/Frame.cpp
+++ b/cldc/src/vm/share/runtime/Frame.cpp
@@ -369,6 +369,18 @@
   Method::Raw method = this->method();
   int bci = this->bci();
 
+  if (callee->is_null()) {
+    // Compiled code runs monitorenter and monitorexit in a shared stub,
+    // after taking the object off the expression stack. A frame stopped
+    // there is waiting in that stub, which finishes the bytecode before it
+    // returns to the deoptimization entry: continue after the bytecode
+    // instead of running it a second time without its operand.
+    const Bytecodes::Code bc = method().bytecode_at(bci);
+    if (bc == Bytecodes::_monitorenter || bc == Bytecodes::_monitorexit) {
+      bci += Bytecodes::length_for(bc);
+    }
+  }
+
 #if 0 && USE_INDIRECT_EXECUTION_SENSOR_UPDATE
   // IMPL_NOTE: if this is ncessary here?
   CompiledMethod::Raw cm = method().compiled_code();
